PROJECT NAME
Hybrid Creative Agency Website Prototype

PROJECT PURPOSE
Build a high-fidelity, responsive frontend prototype for a French digital, AI and consulting agency. The final production website will later be rebuilt in WordPress with Elementor. The Bolt project must therefore serve as both a functional prototype and a precise implementation reference.

CORE WORKING METHOD
- Work in controlled phases.
- Do not attempt to build the entire website in one generation.
- First create the foundations and a private Style Lab.
- Then build one page family at a time.
- Preserve approved components and avoid redesigning them during later tasks.
- Before making broad architectural changes, explain their impact.
- Never remove an approved section unless explicitly requested.
- Never replace a detailed reference with a generic template.

REFERENCE IMAGE RULES
- Treat every file in /public/references/ as a visual reference.
- Do not display the screenshots themselves as webpage backgrounds.
- Do not flatten a section into an image.
- Rebuild every visible element as real semantic HTML, CSS and functional JavaScript.
- Use the screenshots to infer layout, hierarchy, proportions, spacing, typography, layering, color usage and interaction patterns.
- Do not copy German or English reference content into the final pages.
- Interface copy and placeholder content must be written in French.
- Third-party logos and reference brands are visual placeholders only.

DESIGN DIRECTION
Create a controlled hybrid between two visual systems.

ESPRIT 01:
- Dark editorial art direction.
- Black and charcoal backgrounds.
- Visible vertical grid lines.
- Oversized condensed typography.
- Large outlined words using text stroke.
- Asymmetric project compositions.
- Images overlapping titles.
- Vertical numbering and side markers.
- Minimal white buttons.
- Premium creative-studio feeling.
- Magazine-inspired About page layouts.

ESPRIT 02:
- White and vivid colored sections.
- Geometric bold typography.
- Large amounts of negative space.
- Full-height or near-full-height sections.
- Split layouts with editorial text and large product imagery.
- Horizontal case-study slider.
- Persistent vertical side label.
- Persistent social rail.
- Bright blue, purple, magenta, red and green backgrounds.
- Modular service presentation.
- Editorial Insights grid.
- Large process call-to-action sections.

HYBRIDIZATION RULES
- Do not randomly mix styles inside the same component.
- Each component must have a clearly defined visual variant.
- Reuse one consistent spacing, typography and layout system across both directions.
- Preserve dark and light variants in the Style Lab so a human can compare and select them.
- Use color strategically, not decoratively.
- Maintain a premium, confident and editorial identity.

TECHNICAL DIRECTION
- Use React, TypeScript and Vite.
- Use React Router for pages.
- Use semantic HTML5.
- Use reusable React components.
- Use plain CSS, CSS Modules or well-organized SCSS.
- Use CSS custom properties for all design tokens.
- Do not use Tailwind.
- Do not use a heavy UI component library.
- Do not add a backend or database.
- Store placeholder content in typed data files rather than scattering it through components.
- Prefer CSS Grid and Flexbox.
- Use absolute positioning only for deliberate editorial overlays.
- Avoid WebGL, canvas-based layouts and effects that will be difficult to reproduce in Elementor.
- Use lightweight native interactions and IntersectionObserver where needed.
- Avoid large animation dependencies unless strictly necessary.

WORDPRESS AND ELEMENTOR CONSTRAINTS
- The final site will be rebuilt using WordPress and Elementor.
- Keep section structures understandable and transferable to Elementor containers.
- Use consistent max-width containers.
- Use a limited spacing scale.
- Use global color and typography tokens.
- Make recurring sections reusable.
- Clearly identify content that should later become WordPress dynamic content.
- Projects, case studies, portfolio entries, expertise pages and blog articles must be modeled as reusable data-driven templates.
- Produce an ELEMENTOR_HANDOFF.md document.
- Do not generate WordPress or Elementor code unless explicitly requested.

RESPONSIVE REQUIREMENTS
The website is a responsive website, not a native mobile application.

Required reference widths:
- 1920px
- 1440px
- 1024px
- 768px
- 390px

Desktop screenshots are the visual source of truth.
No mobile screenshots are available, so create a coherent mobile interpretation that preserves hierarchy and identity.

FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
- Every button and link must be clickable from the first build.
- Navigation must work.
- The desktop and mobile menus must work.
- Sliders must work with buttons, drag or touch, keyboard access and pagination.
- Scroll arrows must move to the intended section.
- Back-to-top buttons must work.
- Filters and tabs must work.
- Contact forms must validate and display a simulated success state.
- Modal elements must open and close correctly.
- All interactive elements need hover, focus and active states.
- External social links may temporarily use safe placeholder URLs.

ACCESSIBILITY
- Semantic heading structure.
- Keyboard-accessible navigation and interactions.
- Visible focus states.
- Descriptive image alt text.
- Touch targets of at least 44 by 44 pixels.
- Sufficient text contrast.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion.
- Do not rely on color alone to communicate meaning.

PERFORMANCE
- Lazy-load non-critical images.
- Prevent avoidable layout shifts.
- Use responsive images.
- Keep dependencies minimal.
- Avoid autoplaying heavy media.
- Use placeholder assets without generating unnecessary AI images.

QUALITY RULE
The result must feel intentionally art-directed, not like a generic SaaS template.